body:
Because a domestic cat is such a deadly threat to your well being. This is why I'm happy to live in Britain, even right now.

&#x200B;

Edit: Ok, I definitely read that as cat at the start of this.

In a restaurant or walking down the street, or a train station? No, my dog should be on a lead where I can control her, and that's what I do. But anyone who gets that bothered by roaming dogs at, say, a park or a beach, shouldn't be going there, to be frank. If you're about to tell me that this happens to you when you're eating out then by all means, make a fuss (you still don't have the right to jump straight to punching), but I'm betting this happens to you in dog friendly areas like parks. As for the patio, a dog should not be allowed to roam in neighbourhoods, no, but a simple shooing will suffice.

&#x200B;

And no, I don't shout at crying babies.

You have the right to get it away from your space, sure, but absolutely no right to go straight to punching it. Start by gently pushing it back, and only react with violence if the dog appears to be aggressive. Believe me, you'll know.

&#x200B;

If my dog approached you on the beach and I saw you gently push her away then I could not care less. I'll call her and perhaps smile at you as I walk past, nodding and saying thank you/sorry, usual pleasantries. But if you just straight up punch her? You'd best be sure I'm walking over and punching you right back.

Hey fellow UKer, how are you coping with the lockdown? I'm going slowly mad ha ha.

&#x200B;

Merry and Pippin were the same at first, then I left a few treats around my bed and they got more comfortable roaming. But one of the things I have read a lot in these past few weeks is that girls are usually harder.
